WEBVTT

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:03.148
Clauses and complete sentences.

00:00:03.148 --> 00:00:06.248
Every sentence needs a subject and a main verb.

00:00:06.248 --> 00:00:09.851
Get this, and half the punctuation questions get easier.

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.211
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:13.211 --> 00:00:19.236
The choices use the same words, with different punctuation, or a verb in different forms.

00:00:19.236 --> 00:00:22.829
And the question asks about the conventions of Standard English.

00:00:23.881 --> 00:00:28.178
It's testing one thing: where a complete sentence starts, and where it stops.

00:00:29.211 --> 00:00:30.461
Here's the method.

00:00:30.461 --> 00:00:32.246
First, find the subject.

00:00:32.246 --> 00:00:33.836
Who or what is this about?

00:00:33.836 --> 00:00:35.661
Then find its main verb.

00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:38.841
Words like stretching, or to stretch, don't count.

00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:40.866
Now test each side of the blank.

00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:43.241
Could it stand alone as a sentence?

00:00:43.241 --> 00:00:50.725
If both sides are full sentences, they need a real break: a period, a semicolon, or a comma with a FANBOYS word.

00:00:51.806 --> 00:00:55.456
Rule one: a sentence needs a subject and a main verb.

00:00:55.456 --> 00:00:57.756
The storm ending isn't a sentence.

00:00:57.756 --> 00:00:59.566
Ending can't be a main verb.

00:00:59.566 --> 00:01:00.941
The storm ended is.

00:01:02.006 --> 00:01:07.001
Rule two: words like because, although, and which make a clause incomplete.

00:01:07.001 --> 00:01:10.071
Because it rained, on its own, is a fragment.

00:01:10.071 --> 00:01:12.203
It needs a full sentence attached.

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:17.623
Rule three: a comma alone can never join two full sentences.

00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:19.548
That's called a comma splice.

00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:24.097
Use a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a FANBOYS word.

00:01:25.148 --> 00:01:26.373
Let's try one.

00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:29.348
The subject is the Atacama Desert in Chile.

00:01:29.348 --> 00:01:32.223
The part between the commas is extra description.

00:01:32.223 --> 00:01:33.693
So where's the main verb?

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:34.888
There isn't one yet.

00:01:34.888 --> 00:01:36.788
The blank has to supply it.

00:01:36.788 --> 00:01:38.148
Now the choices.

00:01:38.148 --> 00:01:39.113
Receiving.

00:01:39.113 --> 00:01:40.358
Having received.

00:01:40.358 --> 00:01:41.583
To receive.

00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:43.483
None of those can be a main verb.

00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:47.663
Receives is a real main verb, and now the sentence is complete.

00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:49.018
The answer is A.

00:01:50.106 --> 00:01:52.281
Watch out for the long fragment.

00:01:52.281 --> 00:01:56.001
A sentence can go on and on, and still have no main verb.

00:01:56.001 --> 00:01:58.176
This one never says what Petra does.

00:01:58.176 --> 00:02:00.413
Add was, and now it's complete.

00:02:01.444 --> 00:02:03.314
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:02:03.314 --> 00:02:04.374
How to spot it.

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:05.724
The four steps.

00:02:05.724 --> 00:02:06.829
The rules.

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:07.854
And the trap.

00:02:07.854 --> 00:02:10.613
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
